data.content:
## The Problem: Missing `await`

TUnit assertions are **async and must be awaited**. Without `await`, the assertion is never actually executed — it returns a task that gets silently discarded, so the test always passes.

```csharp
// ❌ Bug: assertion never runs
Assert.That(total).IsEqualTo(42m);

// ✅ Fix: await the assertion
await Assert.That(total).IsEqualTo(42m);
```

## The Fix

```csharp
[Test]
public async Task CalculateTotal_TwoItems_ReturnsSum()
{
    var cart = new Cart();
    cart.Add(new Item(price: 10m));
    cart.Add(new Item(price: 32m));

    var total = cart.CalculateTotal();

    await Assert.That(total).IsEqualTo(42m);
}
```

TUnit ships a built-in Roslyn analyzer that warns on unawaited assertions — make sure you don't have it suppressed. Once you add the `await`, your test will correctly fail and expose the bug.
